{"status":"ok","message-type":"work-list","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"facets":{},"total-results":1043246,"items":[{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2026,6,6]],"date-time":"2026-06-06T07:11:35Z","timestamp":1780729895672,"version":"3.54.1"},"reference-count":46,"publisher":"AIP Publishing","issue":"19","content-domain":{"domain":["pubs.aip.org"],"crossmark-restriction":true},"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2011,11,21]]},"abstract":"<jats:p>We introduce an improvement to the Hubbard U augmented density functional approach known as DFT+U that incorporates variations in the value of self-consistently calculated, linear-response U with changes in geometry. This approach overcomes the one major shortcoming of previous DFT+U studies, i.e., the use of an averaged Hubbard U when comparing energies for different points along a potential energy surface is no longer required. While DFT+U is quite successful at providing accurate descriptions of localized electrons (e.g., d or f) by correcting self-interaction errors of standard exchange correlation functionals, we show several diatomic molecule examples where this position-dependent DFT+\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\begin{document}$U(\\mathbf {R})$\\end{document}U(R) provides a significant two- to four-fold improvement over DFT+U predictions, when compared to accurate correlated quantum chemistry and experimental references. DFT+\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\begin{document}$U(\\mathbf {R})$\\end{document}U(R) reduces errors in binding energies, frequencies, and equilibrium bond lengths by applying the linear-response, position-dependent \\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\begin{document}$U(\\mathbf {R})$\\end{document}U(R) at each configuration considered. This extension is most relevant where variations in U are large across the points being compared, as is the case with covalent diatomic molecules such as transition-metal oxides. We thus provide a tool for deciding whether a standard DFT+U approach is sufficient by determining the strength of the dependence of U on changes in coordinates. We also apply this approach to larger systems with greater degrees of freedom and demonstrate how DFT+\\documentclass[12pt]{minimal}\\begin{document}$U(\\mathbf {R})$\\end{document}U(R) may be applied automatically in relaxations, transition-state finding methods, and dynamics.<\/jats:p>","DOI":"10.1063\/1.3660353","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2011,11,21]],"date-time":"2011-11-21T13:50:56Z","timestamp":1321883456000},"update-policy":"https:\/\/doi.org\/10.1063\/aip-crossmark-policy-page","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":72,"title":["Accurate potential energy surfaces with a DFT+$U(\\mathbf {R})$U(R) approach"],"prefix":"10.1063","volume":"135","author":[{"given":"Heather J.","family":"Kulik","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Stanford University 1 Department of Chemistry, , Stanford, California 94305, USA"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]},{"given":"Nicola","family":"Marzari","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"University of Oxford 2 Department of Materials, , Oxford OX1 3PH, United Kingdom"}],"role":[{"vocabulary":"crossref","role":"author"}]}],"member":"317","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2011,11,18]]},"reference":[{"key":"2023062600014070700_c1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"943","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.44.943","volume":"44","year":"1991","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. B"},{"key":"2023062600014070700_c2","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"R5467","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.52.R5467","volume":"52","year":"1995","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. B"},{"key":"2023062600014070700_c3","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"1505","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.57.1505","volume":"57","year":"1998","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. B"},{"key":"2023062600014070700_c4","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","first-page":"035105","DOI":"10.1103\/PhysRevB.71.035105","volume":"71","year":"2005","journal-title":"Phys. Rev. B"},{"key":"2